WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.292 --> 00:00:03.587
Tim's used the word empowering a lot.

2
00:00:03.587 --> 00:00:05.756
And we actually have a framework,

3
00:00:05.756 --> 00:00:09.552
from a perspective of walking away
with something perhaps tangible.

4
00:00:09.552 --> 00:00:13.422
This framework has actually stood the
test of time with a number of very senior

5
00:00:13.422 --> 00:00:16.684
people in terms of how to
create an empowered culture.

6
00:00:16.684 --> 00:00:17.967
And there's a definition.

7
00:00:17.967 --> 00:00:22.170
It's not ours, but its definition comes
from back in the late '80s where a group

8
00:00:22.170 --> 00:00:25.025
that focused on situational
leadership came up with this.

9
00:00:25.025 --> 00:00:28.279
And the bottom line is,
the definition is very simple.

10
00:00:28.279 --> 00:00:32.712
It says, provide people with the right
level of autonomy that's commensurate with

11
00:00:32.712 --> 00:00:35.035
their ability to do it,
and their desire to do it.

12
00:00:36.579 --> 00:00:38.289
So if you think about it as a graph.

13
00:00:38.289 --> 00:00:42.697
If you really think about autonomy
on one side, then motivation,

14
00:00:42.697 --> 00:00:45.745
that's a desire, the skill,
knowledge, experience,

15
00:00:45.745 --> 00:00:49.623
and then even the capacity to do
it, if you will, on the y-axis,

16
00:00:49.623 --> 00:00:53.429
on the x-axis, then the directly
proportionate line is where people are most

17
00:00:53.429 --> 00:00:54.429
empowered.

18
00:00:54.763 --> 00:01:00.436
And for many leaders, they go, I get it,
but now what do I do with that information?

19
00:01:00.436 --> 00:01:03.819
And how do I act on it? And
there are what we call, sort of,

20
00:01:03.819 --> 00:01:06.722
five archetypical ways
that you, as an individual,

21
00:01:06.722 --> 00:01:09.528
should be thinking about how
you're empowering your teams.

22
00:01:09.528 --> 00:01:14.141
So if the desire or the
will to do it is really low,

23
00:01:14.141 --> 00:01:16.035
then being directive is really good.

24
00:01:17.077 --> 00:01:19.766
In fact, some people say, well,
that's telling people to do things,

25
00:01:19.766 --> 00:01:20.956
you're not empowering them.

26
00:01:20.956 --> 00:01:24.418
No, you are. By coaching or correcting
them, because that's what they need.

27
00:01:24.418 --> 00:01:27.379
And when they have that, and
they know how to do it right,

28
00:01:27.379 --> 00:01:31.827
then they feel more courageous in being
able to do it the next time in the way that

29
00:01:31.827 --> 00:01:33.177
you told them to do it better.

30
00:01:33.177 --> 00:01:35.095
And that's what empowers people.

